\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Discussion}
\title{How much can post-training buy?}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{language-model}
\tag{reinforcement-learning}

\p{Frontier language models are no longer produced by pretraining alone.
Instruction tuning, preference optimization, outcome-verifiable reinforcement
learning, process feedback, tool use, long rollouts, and repeated interaction
with sandboxes can substantially change which behaviours are reliable. The
central FTIP question is not simply whether one recipe improves one benchmark.
It is how capability gain changes when we vary the quality and quantity of
feedback, the cost of producing it, the update rule, the base model, and the
inference protocol.}

\p{This question has two different readings. \strong{Elicitation} moves
probability mass toward successful behaviours already supported by the base
policy. \strong{Acquisition} adds independently validated successful
strategies, transfers them to new tasks, or makes them available under a fixed
inference budget when the base policy had negligible support. Pass-at-one,
majority vote, pass-at-#{k}, support coverage, and transfer therefore answer
different questions. The \citef{shao2024deepseekmath} analysis and the later
\citef{guo2025deepseek} experiments motivate this distinction, but do not by
themselves give a universal test for acquisition.}

\p{A second distinction is between allocated and realized compute. A protocol
may be allowed a large rollout or inference budget but fail to use it
effectively; it may also reuse a costly rollout for several gradient updates,
or predict a later parameter state from an early checkpoint trajectory. Thus
“training steps,” generated tokens, verifier calls, optimizer FLOPs, sandbox
time, and wall-clock time are not interchangeable units.}

\p{The intended endpoint is a family of conditional scaling laws and limit
theorems, not an absolute upper bound on “intelligence.” The immediate task is
more modest: define the objects well enough that claims about signal,
coverage, compute, and capability have an unambiguous semantic type.}
